# New Branch From Main

When starting work on a new task that needs its own branch, always create the branch from the **latest `main`** — not from whatever branch happens to be checked out.

Skip this rule **only** if the user explicitly says otherwise (e.g. "branch off `feat/tabs`", "stack this on top of my current branch").

## Steps to follow every time

1. **Fetch the latest state of the remote**
   ```
   git fetch origin
   ```

2. **Create the new branch from `origin/main`**
   ```
   git switch -c <branch-name> origin/main
   ```
   Use a Conventional-Commits-style prefix matching the work type:
   `feat/…`, `fix/…`, `chore/…`, `refactor/…`, `docs/…`.

3. **Verify the base**
   ```
   git log --oneline -1
   ```
   The first commit shown must match the tip of `origin/main`. If it does not, stop and tell the user — do not start coding on the wrong base.

## Hard rules

- NEVER branch off the currently checked-out feature branch by accident. Always pass `origin/main` (or `main`, if it tracks `origin/main`) explicitly to `git switch -c` / `git checkout -b`.
- NEVER skip the `git fetch origin` step — a stale local `main` produces an out-of-date base.
- If the working tree has uncommitted changes when the user asks for a new branch, stop and ask whether to stash, commit, or discard them before branching.
- If the user explicitly says to branch off something other than `main`, follow their instruction and confirm the base in your next message.
